<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abbad II al-Mu'tadid</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1042">1042</a>–<a href="/years/1069">1069</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as al-Mutadid of Seville</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abbad II <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutadid">al-Mu'tadid</a> was the second Abbadid ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">Taifa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, reigning from 1042 to 1069. Ruthless and ambitious, he made Seville the strongest taifa of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> by absorbing smaller neighbours, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huelva">Huelva</a> in 1051 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silves">Silves</a> in 1063. His son al-Mu'tamid, the poet-king, succeeded him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-mutadid-of-seville.jpg" alt="Abbad II al-Mu'tadid" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Numismática Pliego · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dinar_almotadid_21881.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1058 <b>al-Mutadid</b> of the Taifa of Seville besieged Algeciras and forced al-Qasim ibn Muhammad to surrender it.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-algeciras">Taifa of Algeciras</a></li><li><blockquote>The Abbadid ruler <b>al-Mutadid</b> of the Taifa of Seville annexed Arcos in the 1050s or 1060s as he swept up the Berber principalities of the south-west.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-arcos">Taifa of Arcos</a></li><li><blockquote>After long wars, <b>al-Mutadid</b> of the Taifa of Seville took Carmona in 1067, and the Birzalid family was eclipsed.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-carmona">Taifa of Carmona</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1051 the Abbadid ruler <b>al-Mutadid of Seville</b> forced the last ruler, Abd al-Aziz al-Bakri, to give up Huelva and Saltés.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-huelva">Taifa of Huelva</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1044 <b>al-Mutadid</b> of the Taifa of Seville annexed Mértola.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-mertola">Taifa of Mértola</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1066 <b>al-Mutadid</b> of the Taifa of Seville annexed Morón, reportedly after luring Berber rulers into a trap.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-moron">Taifa of Morón</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.39,-6&amp;year=1055" aria-label="Seville on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4400 818 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Abbad II al-Mu'tadid"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4850 1088h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4850 1088h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.39,-6&amp;year=1055">What was here in 1055? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
